<section class="content">

<h2>Notes for the Catechist</h2>

<p><strong>1. Freedom is not license.</strong> The most common misunderstanding students bring is that freedom means doing whatever you want. This is the single most important point to address. Use the addiction analogy: a drug addict "wants" drugs, but is that freedom? Freedom is ordered toward the good.</p>

<p><strong>2. Avoid the opposite error: moralism.</strong> Don't present God's law as a cage. The analogy of guardrails on a mountain road is effective: they don't restrict your journey — they make it possible. The commandments are the conditions for freedom, not its enemy.</p>

<p><strong>3. Distinguish freedom FROM vs. freedom FOR.</strong> Students often think of freedom as freedom <em>from</em> (rules, parents, authority). Christian freedom is freedom <em>for</em> — for love, for service, for becoming who God made you to be. "For freedom Christ has set us free" (Gal 5:1).</p>

<p><strong>4. Address the "freedom vs. authority" tension honestly.</strong> Don't dodge hard questions. Acknowledge that the Church has sometimes failed to respect freedom (e.g., forced conversions, the Inquisition). At the same time, show that the Church's current teaching on religious liberty (Dignitatis Humanae) is one of the most robust defenses of human freedom in any institution.</p>

<p><strong>5. Use the witnesses.</strong> The stories of Thomas More, Bonhoeffer, and Solzhenitsyn are incredibly powerful. Young people respond to real people who suffered for freedom. They make the concept concrete and heroic.</p>

<p><strong>6. Connect to daily life.</strong> Ask: "What enslaves you?" Social media addiction, fear of others' opinions, resentment, laziness — these are the modern chains. The lesson on freedom should feel personal, not theoretical.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Adaptation by Age</h2>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--semilla">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">🌱 Ages 6-8 &mdash; Seed</h4>
<p>Use simple stories: a bird who can fly anywhere vs. a bird in a cage. Jesus wants us to be free like the bird who flies. Rules from Mom and Dad are like guardrails — they keep us safe so we can play freely. Emphasize that God gives us choices because he loves us.</p>
</div>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--brotes">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">🌿 Ages 9-11 &mdash; Sprouts</h4>
<p>Introduce the concept of true vs. false freedom with concrete examples. What's the difference between choosing to do your homework and being forced? Between choosing not to hit someone and being afraid of punishment? Present the guardrail analogy. Tell the story of St. Thomas More in an age-appropriate way.</p>
</div>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--raiz">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">🪴 Ages 12-14 &mdash; Root</h4>
<p>Full lesson as written. Discuss impediments to freedom (ignorance, fear, habit, force). Introduce Dignitatis Humanae. Explore Galatians 5 in detail. Discuss the tension between individual freedom and the common good. Address peer pressure and social media as modern impediments.</p>
</div>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--arbol">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">🌳 Ages 15+ &mdash; Tree</h4>
<p>Deep theological exploration. Augustine's libertas maior and libertas minor. Veritatis Splendor and the relationship between freedom and truth. Existentialist and utilitarian concepts of freedom vs. the Christian understanding. Bonhoeffer's "cheap grace vs. costly grace." Connect freedom to vocation: freedom is for something, not from something.</p>
</div>

</section>

