<section class="content">

<h2>Difficult Questions</h2>

<p><strong>Q: "If God wants us to be free, why does he give us so many rules?"</strong><br/>
A: The rules are not arbitrary restrictions — they are the conditions for freedom. Just as the rules of the road (stop signs, speed limits) make it possible for everyone to drive freely and safely, God's commandments make it possible for us to live freely and well. Without rules, there is chaos, not freedom.</p>

<p><strong>Q: "What about people who have never heard of Christ? Are they not free?"</strong><br/>
A: The Church teaches that every person has access to natural moral law (CCC 1954) — the basic sense of right and wrong written on the human heart. Grace works even where the Gospel hasn't been explicitly proclaimed. But the fullness of freedom comes through Christ, who reveals both the truth about God and the truth about ourselves.</p>

<p><strong>Q: "Does the Church really believe in religious freedom? What about forced conversions in history?"</strong><br/>
A: The Church acknowledges that there have been grave violations of religious freedom in her history, including forced conversions. These were sins against the dignity of the human person and against the nature of faith, which must be free. The Church's current teaching, articulated in Dignitatis Humanae, is one of the strongest defenses of religious freedom in any institution's teaching.</p>

<p><strong>Q: "What if my conscience tells me something is okay but the Church says it's wrong?"</strong><br/>
A: This is a crucial question about the relationship between conscience and authority — and it's the subject of our next lesson! For now: a conscience must be formed, not just followed blindly. And formation includes listening to the Church, which is a trusted guide. But we'll explore this much more deeply next time.</p>

<p><strong>Q: "How is addiction related to freedom?"</strong><br/>
A: Addiction is one of the clearest examples of diminished freedom (CCC 1735). A person with a genuine addiction does not have the same degree of voluntary control as someone without it. This doesn't mean they have zero responsibility, but it does mean their culpability may be reduced. The Church recognizes this and approaches addiction with compassion, not condemnation — while still encouraging the person to seek the freedom that Christ offers.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Catechist's Prayer</h2>

<p>
Lord Jesus, who said "the truth will set you free," give me the wisdom to teach the truth about freedom with clarity and love. Help me never to present your commandments as burdens but as the path to authentic liberty. Give my students the courage to choose the good even when it costs them, and the joy that comes from living in the truth.</p>

<p>St. Thomas More, patron of conscience, pray for us.<br/>
St. John Paul II, prophet of freedom, pray for us.</p>

<p><em>Amen.</em></p>

</section>

